\description{
\code{vglm_markov()} is the recommended VGAM fitting entrypoint for
\code{mostr} SOP workflows. It follows \code{VGAM::vglm()} closely, stores
separate likelihood, refit, and designated-start profile data for downstream
SOP inference, and marks the fit so the SOP prediction code can use
package-native Markov prediction. For inline
registered RMS spline terms such as \code{rcs(time, 4)}, \code{lsp(time, 5)}, or their
namespace-qualified forms, the internal VGAM assignment metadata is split by
generated spline column.
This makes it possible to give individual spline basis columns separate
constraint matrices for partial proportional odds models. Inline splines may
be used for time or for numeric previous-state effects such as
\code{rcs(yprev, 6)}.
}
\details{
\code{vglm_markov()} also makes the registered \code{rms} formula helpers \code{rcs()},
\code{lsp()}, and \verb{\%ia\%}
available while building model frames and prediction design matrices. This
lets formulas use \code{time \%ia\% yprev} for a linear-time by previous-state
interaction without requiring \code{library(rms)} in the user's session.

Non-spline terms, factor terms, and workflows that use explicit precomputed
basis columns keep ordinary VGAM assignment behavior. When \code{id_var} is
supplied, the returned object is wrapped with \code{\link[=robcov_vglm]{robcov_vglm()}} using that
patient/cluster ID. When \code{id_var} is omitted, a plain S4 \code{vglm} object is
returned; if \code{data} contains an \code{id} column, a warning reminds the user that
automatic cluster-robust covariance and FWB refits need \code{id_var}.

Offsets are not supported in \code{mostr} SOP workflows, so \code{vglm_markov()}
rejects both \code{offset()} terms and the \code{offset} argument.

Starting profiles are retained before response-driven model-frame
omission. Every fitted patient must have exactly one complete profile at
the cohort-wide \code{first_followup_time}, including ID, model predictors,
time, and the previous state; the transition response on that row may be
missing. A patient is included only when at least one usable likelihood
transition is fitted somewhere. Patients with no fitted transition are
excluded, and a fitted patient without a complete designated profile is an
error. Later likelihood rows are not substituted for that profile.
